      "text": "\n\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\nThis phenomenon\u2014high throughput on repeated prompts versus low throughput on unique prompts\u2014is a classic indicator of **inference caching** or **KV-cache reuse**, rather than a reflection of the model\u2019s raw computational speed. Below is a structured analysis of the causes, methods to isolate true performance, and best practices for benchmarking.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Plausible Causes\n\n#### A. KV-Cache Reuse (Most Likely)\n- **Mechanism**: In transformer-based LLMs, the Key-Value (KV) cache stores intermediate computations for each token. If the same prompt is repeated, the server may reuse the pre-computed KV states from the first inference, skipping the expensive prefill phase.\n- **Result**: The \"generation\" phase (token-by-token output) dominates, which is much faster than the \"prefill\" phase (processing the entire input prompt). Repeated prompts thus show artificially high tokens/sec.\n\n#### B. Prompt Caching / Semantic Caching\n- **Mechanism**: Some inference servers (e.g., vLLM, TGI, or custom frameworks) implement explicit caching:\n  - **Exact Match Cache**: If the prompt string is identical, the full response may be cached and returned instantly.\n  - **Semantic Cache**: Similar prompts may map to cached responses.\n- **Result**: Near-zero compute time for repeated prompts, leading to inflated throughput metrics.\n\n#### C. Batch Processing Artifacts\n- **Mechanism**: If the benchmark sends repeated prompts in a batch, the server may optimize memory access patterns or reuse internal buffers more efficiently than with diverse prompts.\n- **Result**: Higher effective throughput due to reduced memory bandwidth contention.\n\n#### D. Warm-Up Effects\n- **Mechanism**: The first inference call triggers JIT compilation, memory allocation, and model loading. Subsequent calls benefit from a \"warm\" state.\n- **Result**: If the benchmark does not properly discard warm-up iterations, early unique prompts may be slower, while later repeated prompts benefit from both warm-up and caching.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Separating Valid Fresh-Response Throughput from Warmed/Cached Throughput\n\nTo measure **true inference performance**, you must ensure that every prompt is processed from scratch, without benefit from prior computations.\n\n#### A. Use Unique, Non-Overlapping Prompts\n- **Strategy**: Generate a large set of distinct prompts (e.g., using random text, diverse topics, or synthetic data) to",
      "text_preview": "\n\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\nThis phenomenon\u2014high throughput on repeated prompts versus low throughput on unique prompts\u2014is a classic indicator of **inference caching** or **KV-cache reuse**, rather than a reflection of the model\u2019s raw computational speed. Below is a structured analysis of the causes, methods to isolate true p",
