      "stream_token_id_count": 512,
      "text": "\n\nThis is a classic systems design trade-off. The choice between **Tensor Parallelism (TP)** and **Data Parallelism (DP)** (independent replicas) depends almost entirely on whether your bottleneck is **latency** (time to first token / inter-token latency) or **throughput** (total tokens per second across all users).\n\nHere is a detailed comparison across the dimensions you specified.\n\n### 1. Latency (TTFT & ITL)\n\n*   **Tensor Parallel (TP=4):**\n    *   **Time to First Token (TTFT):** Significantly lower. The prompt processing (prefill) phase is compute-bound. Splitting the matrix multiplications across 4 GPUs allows the prompt to be processed in roughly 1/4th the time (minus communication overhead).\n    *   **Inter-Token Latency (ITL):** Lower. During the decode phase, each GPU holds a slice of the weights. The computation per step is faster, though it is bottlenecked by the synchronization required at every single token step.\n    *   **Verdict:** Best for interactive, real-time applications (chatbots, coding assistants) where a user is waiting for the next word.\n\n*   **Independent Replicas (DP=4):**\n    *   **TTFT:** High. Each replica processes the entire prompt on a single GPU. If the prompt is long, this is slow.\n    *   **ITL:** High. The decode step is limited by the memory bandwidth of a single GPU.\n    *   **Verdict:** Poor for single-user interactive latency. However, if you have 4 concurrent users, each gets a dedicated GPU, so their *perceived* latency is the same as a single-GPU inference, but they don't queue behind each other.\n\n### 2. Throughput (Tokens/Second)\n\n*   **Tensor Parallel (TP=4):**\n    *   **Aggregate Throughput:** Lower. Because every token generation requires synchronizing all 4 GPUs (All-Reduce operations), the system is limited by the slowest link and the communication overhead. You cannot easily batch large numbers of independent requests without complex pipeline parallelism.\n    *   **Verdict:** Optimized for low-latency, low-batch-size workloads.\n\n*   **Independent Replicas (DP=4):**\n    *   **Aggregate Throughput:** Higher. Each GPU can independently batch requests. If you have 40 concurrent users",
      "text_preview": "\n\nThis is a classic systems design trade-off. The choice between **Tensor Parallelism (TP)** and **Data Parallelism (DP)** (independent replicas) depends almost entirely on whether your bottleneck is **latency** (time to first token / inter-token latency) or **throughput** (total tokens per second across all users).\n\nH",
