      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a technical guide for adding a new benchmark to an existing optimization repo. This is a practical, structured document. Let me think about what makes a good optimization benchmark repo structure and what the key concerns are:\n\n1. Directory structure - where scripts, data, notes, patches, results go\n2. Naming conventions for run directories\n3. Reproducibility requirements\n4. Validation criteria for promoting results\n\nLet me think about what a real optimization repo looks like. Think of repos like:\n- Compiler optimization benchmarks\n- Algorithm optimization repos\n- Performance tuning repos\n\nThe structure should be practical and opinionated. Let me write this as a clear, actionable guide with specific conventions. I'll make it feel like an internal engineering document - direct, specific, no fluff.\n\nLet me structure it:\n- Overview / philosophy\n- Directory layout\n- Naming conventions\n- What to record (reproducibility)\n- Validation / promotion criteria\n- Checklist\n\nI'll make it concrete with examples rather than abstract. Let me write it in a way that's immediately usable.\n</think>\n\n# Adding a New Benchmark to the Optimization Repo\n\n## 1. Directory Layout\n\nEvery benchmark lives under `benchmarks/<name>/`. Create the following skeleton before writing a single line of code:\n\n```\nbenchmarks/<name>/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 README.md            # What, why, how to run, expected output\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 run.sh               # Single entry point (bash or zsh)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 src/                 # Benchmark source (the thing being measured)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 data/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 inputs/          # Fixed input files (committed, \u2264 50 MB total)\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 generated/       # .gitkeep only; generated at run time\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 patches/             # Candidate patches under evaluation (one per file)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 notes/\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 <date>-<topic>.md   # Scratch analysis, hypotheses, dead ends\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 results/             # One subdirectory per run (see \u00a72)\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 Makefile             # Optional; wraps run.sh for convenience\n```\n\nRules:\n\n- **`run.sh` is the only thing CI or a collaborator should ever invoke.** It must be idempotent: running it twice produces the same output and does not corrupt prior results.\n- **`data/inputs/` is committed.** If an input",
