      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a risk register for switching a production inference endpoint from one runtime to another. They want at least 8 risks with specific columns: severity, likelihood, detection method, mitigation, and rollback trigger. They explicitly want real operational and quality risks, not generic PM filler.\n\nLet me think about what actually goes wrong when you switch inference runtimes in production. I need to think like an ML engineer / SRE who has actually done this.\n\nReal risks:\n\n1. **Numerical precision / floating-point differences** - Different runtimes (e.g., PyTorch vs ONNX Runtime, or different CUDA versions, or TensorRT vs native) can produce slightly different outputs due to different kernel implementations, different reduction orders, different precision handling. This can cause model outputs to drift, especially for models with many layers where small differences compound. Detection: A/B shadow traffic comparing outputs. Mitigation: Set tolerance thresholds, use deterministic kernels where possible. Rollback trigger: output divergence exceeds threshold.\n\n2. **Latency regression** - The new runtime might be slower for certain input shapes, batch sizes, or on specific hardware. The old runtime might have had optimized kernels for your specific model architecture. Detection: p50/p95/p99 latency monitoring, per-request timing. Mitigation: Load test with production traffic distribution before cutover. Rollback trigger: p99 latency exceeds SLO.\n\n3. **Memory footprint / OOM** - Different runtimes allocate memory differently. The new runtime might use more VRAM/RAM, or have different memory management patterns (e.g., fragmentation, allocator behavior). Under load, this could cause OOM kills. Detection: GPU memory monitoring, cgroup memory limits. Mitigation: Pre-test with peak load, set memory limits with headroom. Rollback trigger: memory usage exceeds threshold or OOM events.\n\n4. **Batching / concurrency behavior differences** - The old runtime might handle concurrent requests differently. Dynamic batching behavior, request queuing, or thread pool behavior might differ. Under burst traffic, the new runtime might queue differently, causing some requests to time out while others are fast. Detection: request queue depth monitoring, per-request latency distribution under load. Mitigation: Stress test with burst patterns matching production. Rollback trigger: queue depth or timeout rate exceeds threshold.\n\n5. **Model artifact compatibility / silent degradation** - The model file might load but produce subtly wrong outputs. E.g., a quantized model loaded",
      "text_preview": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a risk register for switching a production inference endpoint from one runtime to another. They want at least 8 risks with specific columns: severity, likelihood, detection method, mitigation, and rollback trigger. They explicitly want real operational and quality risks, not generic PM filler.\n",
