      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user is asking me to write a decision memo about whether to keep optimizing a current model or switch to a different model family. This is a technical/strategic document that should be structured as a proper memo. Let me think about what makes a good decision memo in this context:\n\n1. It needs to be concrete and specific - not generic. I should invent a plausible scenario (e.g., a team working on a specific ML task) to make it feel real and useful.\n2. It should include all the requested elements:\n   - Current evidence (what we know so far)\n   - Opportunity cost (what we're giving up by staying)\n   - Criteria for stopping (when do we know we should switch)\n   - Expected next experiments (what we'll try before deciding)\n   - Quality constraints (non-negotiable requirements)\n   - Artifacts to preserve (what to save for future work)\n\nLet me make this feel like a real internal memo from a ML team. I'll pick a specific scenario: a team working on a document classification/extraction task where they've been iterating on a fine-tuned BERT-based model and are considering switching to a larger transformer family (like LLaMA or Mistral) or a different architecture.\n\nActually, let me make it more specific and realistic. Let's say:\n- Current model: Fine-tuned DeBERTa-v3-base for a multi-label document classification task\n- Alternative: Switching to a fine-tuned Mistral-7B or a larger encoder-decoder\n- The team has been at this for ~6 weeks\n- They're hitting diminishing returns\n\nLet me structure this as a proper memo with clear sections, decision framing, and actionable items. I'll make it feel like it was written by a senior ML engineer or tech lead to their team and stakeholders.\n\nFormat: Memo header, then sections. I'll use clear headings, bullet points where appropriate, and make it feel like a real document with specific numbers and dates.\n</think>\n\n# Decision Memo: Continue Optimizing DeBERTa-v3 Pipeline vs. Switch to Mistral-7B Fine-Tune\n\n**To:** NLP Platform Team, Product Eng (Doc Intelligence), ML Ops\n**From:** R. Vasquez, Senior ML Engineer\n**Date:** 2025-06-14\n**Re:** Go/No-Go on model family switch \u2014 Week 7 checkpoint\n**Classification",
